@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix planning: <http://example.org/orexis/planning#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

<> a orexis:ActionGraph .    # this document is one graph, and this is what it is

#  THE SEARCH'S OWN MOVE: doing nothing while the agent's predictions move the world (#920,
#  knowledge/domain/planning/wait.md). A van waits for another to pass, a grower for the forecast
#  rain instead of a dose, a bidder for the next round to open — every world has it, so it is the
#  planning package's and ships beside its ontology, read by every agent at boot as the package's
#  documents are, and imported by no world.
#
#  IT STATES NO PRECONDITION, NO EFFECT, NO LANDING AND NO IMPLEMENTATION, and each absence is the
#  kernel's to read for this one action alone. A world admits a wait where a ground holding something
#  else, as the search reads it, is laid after the one the world stands in, and it lands where that
#  ground begins (`agent/planning/next_ground.py`), so one wait spans any stretch in which nothing read
#  is predicted to change and none is offered where nothing is predicted at all. It changes nothing of its own, and its world is the later ground with the
#  path replayed there, a move though no effect ran (`agent/planning/take.py`); its step predicts
#  nothing and is answered at its landing; taking it sends nothing. It is in every scope, since it
#  touches no atom (`agent/planning/scope_actions.py`). Any other action whose effect comes to
#  nothing is no move, so this node is the one way a plan lets time pass.
#
#  ITS COST, A TENTH OF THE CHEAPEST ACT ANY DOMAIN SHIPS: more than nothing, so a plan is never
#  padded with a wait that buys nothing, and less than an act, so standing still is chosen where it
#  meets the want as well as acting would. Asked over the world it is taken in, as any action's is.

planning:Wait a orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "wait for the next ground" ;
    planning:costs """SELECT ?cost WHERE { BIND(0.1 AS ?cost) }""" .
